  "worked_solution": "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{3}+2x+1}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)+1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cancel{({x}^{2}+1)}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+C,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{\u2032}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
